1
00:00:00,580 --> 00:00:03,691
A quadrilateral is a simple closed polygon with four

2
00:00:03,691 --> 00:00:07,678
sides. It has four vertices and four angles, and

3
00:00:07,678 --> 00:00:09,771
it also has two diagonals.

4
00:00:10,393 --> 00:00:12,210
In the last lesson we closed a figure with

5
00:00:12,210 --> 00:00:15,944
three line segments: a triangle. What happens if we

6
00:00:15,944 --> 00:00:18,558
add one more corner? Watch the side CA. It

7
00:00:18,558 --> 00:00:22,340
opens out into two new sides, CD and DA,

8
00:00:22,340 --> 00:00:26,723
and the figure closes again. Now the figure has

9
00:00:26,723 --> 00:00:29,824
four sides, so it is a quadrilateral.

10
00:00:30,407 --> 00:00:33,178
Let us build it segment by segment. Here is

11
00:00:33,178 --> 00:00:35,652
side AB. Attach side BC. Attach side CD: the

12
00:00:35,652 --> 00:00:40,273
figure is still open. Attach side DA, and the

13
00:00:40,273 --> 00:00:44,287
ends meet. The figure closes. Simple, because the sides

14
00:00:44,287 --> 00:00:46,847
do not cross; closed, because the ends join up;

15
00:00:46,847 --> 00:00:50,767
and four sides. That is a quadrilateral, and like

16
00:00:50,767 --> 00:00:55,587
every closed figure it has an interior, a boundary

17
00:00:55,587 --> 00:00:56,738
and an exterior.

18
00:00:57,300 --> 00:01:00,300
The four line segments are the sides: side AB,

19
00:01:00,300 --> 00:01:03,210
side BC, side CD, and side DA, which brings

20
00:01:03,210 --> 00:01:07,000
us back to A. The four points A, B,

21
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:09,589
C and D are its vertices. So the sides

22
00:01:09,589 --> 00:01:13,740
form the boundary, and the vertices are the corners

23
00:01:13,740 --> 00:01:15,831
where two sides meet.

24
00:01:16,587 --> 00:01:20,062
At every vertex, the two sides form an angle.

25
00:01:20,062 --> 00:01:23,267
At corner A we have angle A, at corner

26
00:01:23,267 --> 00:01:25,590
B angle B, at corner C angle C, and

27
00:01:25,590 --> 00:01:29,458
at corner D angle D. So four vertices give four angles.

28
00:01:30,080 --> 00:01:33,280
Now compare the sides. Side AB and side BC

29
00:01:33,280 --> 00:01:37,600
meet at the vertex B, so they are adjacent.

30
00:01:37,600 --> 00:01:39,836
Side AB and side DA meet at the vertex

31
00:01:39,836 --> 00:01:44,540
A, so they are adjacent as well. Adjacent sides

32
00:01:44,540 --> 00:01:46,211
always share a vertex.

33
00:01:46,803 --> 00:01:50,578
Some elements are across from each other instead. Side

34
00:01:50,578 --> 00:01:53,743
AB and side CD do not meet at all,

35
00:01:53,743 --> 00:01:57,215
so they are opposite sides. Side BC and side

36
00:01:57,215 --> 00:02:01,923
DA are the other pair of opposite sides. Angles

37
00:02:01,923 --> 00:02:05,477
have opposites too: angle A is opposite angle C,

38
00:02:05,477 --> 00:02:08,990
and angle B is opposite angle D. So adjacent

39
00:02:08,990 --> 00:02:12,034
means next to, and opposite means across.

40
00:02:12,667 --> 00:02:15,937
Now join the opposite vertices. The vertices A and

41
00:02:15,937 --> 00:02:19,311
C are opposite, and joining them gives the segment

42
00:02:19,311 --> 00:02:22,312
AC. The vertices B and D are opposite, and

43
00:02:22,312 --> 00:02:26,227
joining them gives the segment BD. These two segments

44
00:02:26,227 --> 00:02:30,540
are the diagonals of the quadrilateral: a diagonal joins

45
00:02:30,540 --> 00:02:32,278
two opposite vertices.

46
00:02:32,958 --> 00:02:37,064
Let us put the whole relationship map together. Adjacent

47
00:02:37,064 --> 00:02:39,649
elements share a corner: AB and BC share the

48
00:02:39,649 --> 00:02:43,676
vertex B. Opposite elements lie across the shape: AB

49
00:02:43,676 --> 00:02:46,658
and CD share no vertex. And a diagonal connects

50
00:02:46,658 --> 00:02:49,762
the opposite corners: AC joins A and C. So

51
00:02:49,762 --> 00:02:54,198
ask yourself: are they next to each other, across

52
00:02:54,198 --> 00:02:56,309
from each other, or joined across?

53
00:02:56,831 --> 00:03:01,764
Your turn. Four quick checks on quadrilateral PQRS. First:

54
00:03:01,764 --> 00:03:05,111
which side is opposite QR? Second: which angle is

55
00:03:05,111 --> 00:03:08,851
opposite angle P? Third: which sides are adjacent to

56
00:03:08,851 --> 00:03:12,875
PQ? And last of all: join the opposite vertices,

57
00:03:12,875 --> 00:03:14,582
and name the two diagonals.

58
00:03:25,498 --> 00:03:28,798
The side opposite QR is PS. The angle opposite

59
00:03:28,798 --> 00:03:32,318
angle P is angle R. The sides adjacent to

60
00:03:32,318 --> 00:03:36,238
PQ are PS and QR, because each of them

61
00:03:36,238 --> 00:03:40,585
shares a vertex with PQ. And the two diagonals

62
00:03:40,585 --> 00:03:44,118
are PR and QS: they join the opposite vertices

63
00:03:44,118 --> 00:03:46,089
P with R, and Q with S.

64
00:03:46,724 --> 00:03:49,977
One last look. A quadrilateral is a simple closed

65
00:03:49,977 --> 00:03:52,944
polygon with four sides, four vertices and four angles.

66
00:03:52,944 --> 00:03:55,778
Adjacent elements are next to each other, and opposite

67
00:03:55,778 --> 00:03:58,564
elements are across the shape. And its two diagonals

68
00:03:58,564 --> 00:04:01,249
join the opposite vertices. Build it, compare its parts,

69
00:04:01,249 --> 00:04:03,779
and connect the corners. Thank you for watching, and

70
00:04:03,779 --> 00:04:05,195
see you in the next lesson.
